              "youtube_url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dNVjFpHjbDs",
              "review_url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dNVjFpHjbDs&t=70s",
              "start_seconds": 70,
              "end_seconds": 134,
              "label": "Why a bar chart's baseline must be zero",
              "summary": "The instructor displays a bar chart whose y-axis starts at 350 rather than zero. Because readers judge bar charts by comparing bar heights from a shared baseline, the bars appear to show a three-to-four-times increase when the actual change is far smaller. Adding axis labels reveals the deception. The fix is stated plainly: always set the axis to zero.",
              "fact_ids": [
                "f3"
              ],
              "transcript_ref": "dNVjFpHjbDs:70-134"
            }
          ],
          "group_title": "Building and reading bar charts: live code and the zero-baseline rule",
          "bridge": {
            "before": "The first clip shows plt.bar() with string category names as x; the second shows a real misleading chart — watch how the non-zero start distorts apparent differences between bars.",
            "after": "The first clip does not cover the zero-baseline rule or why bar lengths must be true to values; the teaching text fills both gaps."
          },
